1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,816
KV cache compression on the AMD Instinct MI300X.

2
00:00:03,816 --> 00:00:09,011
A four way benchmark of TurboQuant, IsoQuant, PlanarQuant and RotorQuant.

3
00:00:09,011 --> 00:00:13,779
This is a short tour of what we measured, and where the gains stop.

4
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:19,293
Long context decoding is limited by the size of the key value cache.

5
00:00:19,293 --> 00:00:22,413
Three bit compression shrinks it by 4.92 times.

6
00:00:22,413 --> 00:00:26,860
But every method rotates each vector first, and that costs compute.

7
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:29,515
Which one is cheap enough on the MI300X?

8
00:00:30,510 --> 00:00:36,083
A key or value head vector is rotated, quantised to three bits, and stored as a fifty two byte

9
00:00:36,083 --> 00:00:36,439
block.

10
00:00:36,439 --> 00:00:41,123
At decode time a fused kernel unpacks the block and runs attention in one pass.

11
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:45,090
Compression bandwidth, same data, same layout.

12
00:00:45,090 --> 00:00:47,932
IsoQuant packs at 21.8 gigabytes per second.

13
00:00:47,932 --> 00:00:53,743
TurboQuant manages only 2.9, because its rotation needs sixteen thousand multiply adds per

14
00:00:53,743 --> 00:00:54,195
vector.

15
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:01,044
That shows up in prefill compression throughput at thirty two thousand tokens.

16
00:01:01,044 --> 00:01:08,087
PlanarQuant reaches 1,126 thousand tokens per second against 42 thousand for TurboQuant, which

17
00:01:08,087 --> 00:01:09,660
is 26.5 times faster.

18
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:17,381
All four methods store 4.92 times fewer bytes and reconstruct with cosine similarity around

19
00:01:17,381 --> 00:01:20,557
0.983 at three bits and 0.995 at four bits.

20
00:01:20,557 --> 00:01:25,874
Needle in a haystack retrieval passes 100 percent on the synthetic test.

21
00:01:26,870 --> 00:01:34,004
A fused Triton attention kernel that reads the compressed cache directly is slower than fp16 at

22
00:01:34,004 --> 00:01:40,987
short contexts, only 0.28 of fp16 speed at one K, but faster at long contexts: 1.91 at thirty

23
00:01:40,987 --> 00:01:42,639
two K, and up to 2.11.

24
00:01:42,639 --> 00:01:49,398
With grouped query attention, fused decode is 6.3 to 7.5 times faster than decompress then

25
00:01:49,398 --> 00:01:49,923
attend.

26
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:57,832
But in an end to end vLLM run of Mistral seven B, output throughput is flat, between 2,425 and

27
00:01:57,832 --> 00:02:03,348
2,435 tokens per second, whether the cache is fp16, decompressed, or fused.

28
00:02:03,348 --> 00:02:10,187
Weights, the MLP and the framework dominate the step, so the KV format does not unlock decode

29
00:02:10,187 --> 00:02:11,804
speed in that setting.

30
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:13,321
Caveats.

31
00:02:13,321 --> 00:02:16,059
One GPU model, and Mistral seven B shapes.

32
00:02:16,059 --> 00:02:20,165
The needle test is synthetic and perplexity uses small windows.

33
00:02:20,165 --> 00:02:23,488
The serving result is a single batch configuration.

34
00:02:23,488 --> 00:02:26,747
And the fused kernel only wins at longer contexts.

35
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:31,285
Kernels, results, and the full report are in the repo.

